  "id": "Compound::DB00259 - Gene::1576",
  "verbalization": "Sulfanilamide, sulfacytine, and sulfamethoxazole share a common structural feature of being sulfonamide anti-infective agents, which is reflected in their resemblance relationships. These compounds all bind to Cyp3a4, a key enzyme involved in drug metabolism. The evidence suggests that Sulfanilamide, through its resemblance to sulfonamide anti-infective agents, may share a similar mechanism of action with sulfamethoxazole, which is known to bind to Cyp3a4. The lowest common ancestors of these compounds point to the broader class of sulfonamide drugs, reinforcing the idea that Sulfanilamide is part of a family of compounds that interact with Cyp3a4 via a common biochemical pathway. The recurring evidence of structural similarity and enzymatic binding supports a direct relationship between Sulfanilamide and Cyp3a4, mediated through the sulfonamide anti-infective agent class.",
